8. Checking the answer
We split every answer into sentences and check, automatically:

whether the sentence cites any records;
whether the cited records really were among the 8 the AI received (if not, it made them up);
which search step brought the cited records in, and whether search A could have found them.

What a computer cannot check is whether the records really support what the sentence says. That is the job of the human checking.

use only the records given; do not invent records, dates, people or places;
keep what the records show separate from your interpretation;
say when the evidence is too thin, and do not claim a trend from a few records;
end every sentence about the records with the numbers of the records that support it.

What we cannot tell yet

whether each cited record really supports its sentence;
whether the interpretations (for example “a shift from political to commercial contexts”) hold up;
whether answers built on search C are better or worse than those built on search A.

These are exactly the questions the two checkers answer on their sheets. See Human checking.

The tables
The same folder holds three tables for the paper:

table1_queries.csv: the exact search of each question and condition;
table2_conditions.csv: the averages per search (the table at the end of What we found);
table3_visibility.csv: the differences per dimension, with how many questions are beyond chance.
